exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__rightclick_x__context_actions(args: {
  // Max direct actions (default 25).
  limit?: number;
  // Or a semantic type instead of a value.
  type?: string;
  value?: unknown;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```"},{"name":"mcp__rightclick_x__context_compose","description":"RightClick X is a live, self-composing capability graph. Start with context_research (optionally with a topic) to see what this environment can do, context_learn to add any URL, context_compose to turn what you have into what you want across providers, and context_run to execute. The tool list never changes; the graph behind it does.

Give what you have and the type you want; RightClick X discovers routes across the environment and (with run:true) executes, checks and repairs them. Currency/unit refinements require explicit conversions. Attach witnesses for independent business-outcome checks. publish saves a stable goal:<slug> capability another AI can discover with context_graph and invoke with context_run; its checks survive restart and API changes. A successful multi-step pipeline is also saved for reuse.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__rightclick_x__context_compose(args: {
  // If nothing in the graph can do it: research public directories (APIs.guru, MCP Registry), learn the best candidates and re-plan.
  acquire?: boolean;
  // Required to run plans containing destructive capabilities.
  confirm?: boolean;
  // Named inputs, e.g. {"place":"Lisbon","language":"es"} or {"x":{"type":"url","value":"https://..."}}.
  have: { [key: string]: unknown; };
  // Words describing the goal, e.g. 'weather forecast translated'.
  intent?: string;
  // Max pipeline length (default 4).
  maxSteps?: number;
  // After a successful run, save this goal as goal:<slug> in the graph AND as /x/<slug> in the synthesized API. Checks and witnesses are retained and re-run on every invocation. Supply checks/witnesses to establish verified outcomes.
  publish?: string;
  // Optional constraint: plan only with these risk labels, e.g. ["read"].
  risks?: Array<"read" | "write" | "external" | "destructive">;
  // Execute the best plan.
  run?: boolean;
  // Anchors: capability ids or search phrases the pipeline must pass through; the planner wires the types between them.
  through?: Array<string>;
  // Outcome checks on the result, e.g. [{"op":"contains","value":"Lisbon"}, {"op":"gt","path":"temperature","value":-50}]. ops: exists, equals, contains, matches, type, gt, lt, length_gt.
  verify?: Array<{ [key: string]: unknown; }>;
  // Desired semantic type, e.g. text, language, geo.lat, html, hash.
  want: string;
  // Independent outcome checks, repeated for every saved invocation. Discover a read-only checker in the environment. Example: [{"capability":"<checker id>","args":{"order_id":{"input":"order_id"},"tax_amount":{"output":""}},"verify":[{"op":"equals","path":"valid","value":true}]}]. input paths read this invocation's have values; output paths read the candidate result (empty string = whole result); value supplies a literal. The checker must be a separate provider and must pass its predicates. Its contract is pinned on save; a changed checker fails closed.
  witnesses?: Array<{
  args: { [key: string]: {
  // Path in the invocation inputs.
  input: string;
} | {
  // Path in the candidate output; empty string selects all of it.
  output: string;
} | { value: unknown; }; };
  // Exact read-only checker capability id.
  capability: string;
  // Outcome checks on the result, e.g. [{"op":"contains","value":"Lisbon"}, {"op":"gt","path":"temperature","value":-50}]. ops: exists, equals, contains, matches, type, gt, lt, length_gt.
  verify: Array<{ [key: string]: unknown; }>;
}>;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```"},{"name":"mcp__rightclick_x__context_explain","description":"RightClick X is a live, self-composing capability graph. Start with context_research (optionally with a topic) to see what this environment can do, context_learn to add any URL, context_compose to turn what you have into what you want across providers, and context_run to execute. The tool list never changes; the graph behind it does.

